th them as part of this project  something thoughtful that didn't just  reproduce the visual conventions of the  colonial gaze  and in any case i was sometimes  in this research coming across  disturbing and graphic imagery of abuse  and violence including  things that i found in yale collections  again images that i couldn't bring  myself to even save in my database let  alone contemplate publishing  and this was when i came across the work  of the indian artist and researcher  garema gupta based in delhi and i'm  showing you the work here by her that  caused me to reach out to her in the  first place  gutima's work is about the environmental  history of south and southeast asia  she makes drawings observational  drawings of natural objects rocks plants  animals and then she layers them with  drawings of archival materials that  she's found in her own research and she  does this in order to make sense of the  visual and ecological legacies of trade  and migration histories and european  colonial expansion in the region and so  in the work shown here she uses a leaf  of paper from the account books of an  oil palm plantation company in malaysia  as the basis for a drawing of two oil  palm flower heads male and female  and  she creates these layered installations  of drawings in light boxes  um and so you can kind of see the kind  of documentary layer and the  observational  drawing layer kind of merging together  in these light objects  and she does this in order to explore  visually a conflict over land use and  ecological change in the malaysian  archipelago  so i encountered this work by her and i  approached her about a year and a half  ago this was just before the pandemic  went global  and she and i began a conversation about  this project that eventually led to a  full collaboration between us  and so the 10 specimens um that you saw  earlier  eventually became over the course of  2020  10 drawings  and i'm just going to briefly scroll  through all 10 of them