ormer landscape from which the  specimen had been excavated and to the  charts and maps and diagrams that i'd  provided for her and which we talked  about in extensive conversations  um and you know she experimented with  placing the drawings against retraced  maps of the borgot railway cuttings  and so ultimately here is the final work  that she created the the whole work is  on the left and then i've kind of zoomed  in on the specimen on the right  and it's displayed on a light box like i  was explaining earlier so that the kind  of like multiple layers of the image and  therefore the layers of history in this  object could emerge  and so for each of the objects we had a  kind of back and forth like this um and  our my text that i wrote for each one  and the illustrations that garuma  produced were to have developed  collaboratively through that process  and together my words and her  illustrations engage in a kind of  exploratory practice of place making  bringing into view the transformation of  the physical landscape from which the  mineral has come the political and  social landscape through which it has  traveled and the surface of the object  as a landscape of its own  this just another example a close-up of  her drawing of the kimberley mine  diamonds  the coming into being of a natural  history specimen is a transformative  journey in which it sheds a richer sense  of place and its movement through  culture  and through this collaboration we have  tried to help these objects carry their  journeys into the present rather than  leaving them behind  thank you  [Applause]  can you talk a little bit more about um  other  are there other projects like this that  you sort of come across  that you like to take on or other ways  in which you'd like to see this work at  hall i mean it's very exciting that the  exhibitions will actually  come online  yeah  do you mean other projects here at yale  as part of this or do you mean yeah  yeah  yeah yeah absolutely  yeah y