s embarked on voyages across  the globe participating in the global  flows of people and commodities that  underlay british power in a period of  high empire  and finally they were assimilated into a  natural history collection  where they shed some of these pasts in  order to become emblems of nature  apparently outside culture and outside  history they had become mineralogical  specimens  so in this project i was asking what  kind of mineralogy  might embrace and acknowledge these  stories of excavation translocation  transformation and arrival  rather than effacing them in the name of  nature  and i ended up calling this project a  british mineralogy and in this i was  inspired or  more precisely i was provoked  by the london artist and naturalist  james sowerby  in the early 19th century sowerby  published a multi-volume treatise which  he called british mineralogy so no  article just british mineralogy  this was the first major illustrated  mineralogical publication in europe  installed in um or issued in  installments between 1804 and 1817  sowerby's work was visually  groundbreaking with  hand-colored engravings based on  individual mineral specimens  there were two sets of books so first  the first volumes british mineralogy  um were followed a few years later by  their counterpart which was a large  single volume called exotic mineralogy  but sowerby regularly undermined his own  proposed distinction between the british  in this first set of volumes and the  exotic in the final set  in his text the words british english  native  foreign and exotic carried a lot of  weight and there were suggestive  slippages and ambiguities throughout  both the british and the exotic  mineralogy project for sour bee were  limited to reproducing specimens that  were held in london-based mineral  collections which themselves depended of  course upon networks of british  travelers to distant lands  and in his project um even a meteorite  so a piece of stone that was theorized  to have fallen from the heavens an  object that's literally from outer space  um was found to be british it was  included as part of british mineralogy  in the fifth volume sour be observed  what has been thought remarkable is that  this work on british mineralogy should  include so many subjects previously  considered as foreign  so i was looking through sowerby's  drawings and his text while i researched  these ten mineral specimens and i began  to wonder what it would look like to  propose a different kind of british  mineralogy  sowerby's work was an exercise in  drawing the boundaries of locale and  belonging and britishness  in a quite different way than either the  peabody museum or the yale center for  british art did or for that matter  quite different from how a present-day  uk immigration officer would draw those  boundaries  his drawings did not depict the  landscapes from which those specimens  were extracted or the people involved in  doing so although his text did  occasionally describe both the people or  the  landscapes and so it was at this point  that i realized that my project could  not simply be what it had been  originally conceived as which was it was  going to be an exhibition of minerals  accompanied by a text that was grounded  in my historical research into the  objects but the more time i spent with  sowerby the more urgently i felt that  this project required a visual practice  as a part of it a non-textual way of  restoring a multi-layered sense of place  and belonging to each object  and at this point in my research i had  begun to assemble a kind of visual  archive for each mineral as i researched  it so i was finding historical  photographs diagrams maps printed texts  and other documents  many of which were in yale collection  some of which were  things i had access digitally elsewhere  and all the historical images that i'm  showing you today are from this archive  that i assembled  and i felt like something needed to be  done with them as part of this project  something thoughtful that didn't just  reproduce the visual conventions of the  colonial gaze  and in any case i was sometimes  in this research coming across  disturbing and graphic imagery of abuse  and violence including  things that i found in yale collections  again images that i couldn't bring  myself to even save in my database let  alone contemplate publishing  and this was when i came across the work  of the indian artist and researcher  garema gupta based in delhi and i'm  showing you the work here by her that  caused me to reach out to her in the  first place  gutima's work is about the environmental  history of south and southeast asia  she makes drawings observational  drawings of natural objects rocks plants  animals and then she layers them with  drawings of archival materials that  she's found in her own research and she  does this in order to make sense of the  visual and ecological legacies of trade  and migration histories and european  colonial expansion in the region and so  in the work shown here she uses a leaf  of paper from the account books of an  oil palm plantation company in malaysia  as the basis for a drawing of two oil  palm flower heads male and female  and  she creates these layered installations  of drawings in light boxes  um and so you can kind of see the kind  of documentary layer and the  observational  drawing layer kind of merging together  in these light objects  and she does this in order to explore  visually a conflict over land use and  ecological change in the malaysian  archipelago  so i encountered this work by her and i  approached her about a year and a half  ago this was just before the pandemic  went global  and she and i began a conversation about  this project that eventually led to a  full collaboration between us  and so the 10 specimens um that you saw  earlier  eventually became over the course of  2020  10 drawings  and i'm just going to briefly scroll  through all 10 of them right now and  then i'll dive deeply into one of them  before i finish my talk  so i'm going to um  finish with the kind of the rest of my  talk will be telling you the story of  this object from the peabody museum here  one of the ten specimens that i honed in  on for this project and the first one  that i researched  now the only documentation in the  peabody archives for this beautiful  chunk of rock and crystal is the  handwritten label that you can see on it  and that's zoomed in on the right  so the label identifies this mineral as  apophyllite with still bite  and the other information on this label  helps us track the object's origins to a  place called poor got a treacherous pass  in the northern part of the western gods  the western ghats are a mountain range  that run up much of the western coast of  india  these basalt mountains were formed  through violent volcanic eruptions  around 66 million years ago  in the mid-19th century they presented a  natural barrier between the  british-controlled port city of bombay  and the rich resources of the indian  interior  so it was nearly impossible for any kind  of wheeled vehicle to traverse these  mountains and so if the british wanted  indian commodities like silk opium  cotton spices  if they wanted these things to flow  efficiently overseas for trade they  needed a rail line to cut across the  mountains  and so in 1856 the british established  the great india peninsula railway  company and they set out to make it  happen  laying these new rail lines was the  largest and most labor-intensive civil  engineering project of the british  government in india  and it coincided with violent rebellions  that saw the british almost lose control  of northern india in 1857.  the project was carried out over seven  years um 1856 to 1863 and this places it  right around the same time as the  construction of the suez canal  the poor got works actually employed  more workers than the suez project and  ultimately it killed many more people  building this rail line was dangerous  work in precipitous terrain  there were severe extremes of weather in  the mountains and poor sanitation in the  worker camps  ultimately over the seven years of this  project around 25 000 workers died most  of them from cholera which swept every  year through the workers camps  but vast numbers of laborers around 42  000 people a year at the height of this  project migrated seasonally across an  extensive part of western and southern  india to work on the new railway line  they were mostly landless peasants and  tribal people who had been relegated to  the margins of indian society both men  and women  british railway overseers recruited and  organized workers ethnographically  identifying specific caste communities  with specific labor requirements and so  this railway works was also part of a  process that transformed  fluid regional community identities into  hardened stereotypes locked into a  hierarchy of skills and status and a  yearly cycle of migration and contract  work  so these railway works  set a lot of things in motion across the  mountains then  capital commodities and people  and then on a smaller scale they also  sent minerals in motion as well  because blasting tunnels through the  mountains  um in this place exposed dazzling caves  um lined with crystals up to six feet  wide  even today these caves are the world's  most they produce the most spectacular  examples in the world of apophyllite  this kind of crystal that only forms in  this kind of volcanic rock  some of the indian earth workers who  were involved in the railway project  took large crystals from these caves  which the caves became known as the  jewel tunnels  and they embedded them into the mud  walls of their huts in the workers camps  as decoration and as trophy  the chief railway engineer who was an  englishman named james barkley had the  largest specimens saved to distribute to  natural history museums  some of them stayed within india but  most were sent to britain  there they enabled british scientists  and geologists to develop theories for  how crystals like these form following  volcanic disturbances  and one of these samples ended up in the  hands of geologist neville story  masculine who was the keeper of minerals  at the british museum in london his name  is actually on the handwritten label  there you can't really see it but it  says donor ns masculine  he eventually gifted it to yale where it  now resides  so this crystal specimen traveled from  deep inside a mountain in western india  to the british museum in london and then  finally on to new haven  and it did so on a journey shaped by  indian histories of labor migration and  transportation which transformed an  entire landscape and shored up british  imperial control over the indian  subcontinent  so this was the story that i had begun  to delineate for myself around this  object the first one that i researched  as part of this project and i'd  assembled this visual archive that i  mentioned before of photographs and maps  and engravings  and charts  just as the pandemic upended our plans  for garimagarhgupta the artist  the original plan was for her to come to  new haven work closely with me observe  the objects and make art as a result but  amid lockdowns and travel restrictions  we reconceived the project as a remote  engagement between us  mediated by high-resolution digital  photography cloud databases google  documents and zoom  for garma whose work typically involves  extensive fieldwork and travel across  asia  observing and drawing this specimen of  apophyllite in raw involved a real  remaking of her practice she felt her  way into each specimen by zooming deep  into the digital images that the peabody  museum provided  finding in each one a kind of micro  landscape of its own  and when her drawing was complete the  the main observational drawing of the  specimen  she then considered its relation to the  real former landscape from which the  specimen had been excavated and to the  charts and maps and diagrams that i'd  provided for her and which we talked  about in extensive conversations  um and you know she experimented with  placing the drawings against retraced  maps of the borgot railway cuttings  and so ultimately here is the final work  that she created the the whole work is  on the left and then i've kind of zoomed  in on the specimen on the right  and it's displayed on a light box like i  was explaining earlier so that the kind  of like multiple layers of the image and  therefore the layers of history in this  object could emerge  and so for each of the objects we had a  kind of back and forth like this um and  our my text that i wrote for each one  and the illustrations that garuma  produced were to have developed  collaboratively through that process  and together my words and her  illustrations engage in a kind of  exploratory practice of place making  bringing into view the transformation of  the physical landscape from which the  mineral has come the political and  social landscape through which it has  traveled and the surface of the object  as a landscape of its own  this just another example a close-up of  her drawing of the kimberley mine  diamonds  the coming into being of a natural  history specimen is a transformative  journey in which it sheds a richer sense  of place and its movement through  culture  and through this collaboration we have  tried to help these objects carry their  journeys into the present rather than  leaving them behind  thank you  [Applause]  can you talk a little bit more about um  other  are there other projects like this that  you sort of come across  that you like to take on or other ways  in which you'd like to see this work at  hall i mean it's very exciting that the  exhibitions will actually  come online  yeah  do you mean other projects here at yale  as part of this or do you mean yeah  yeah  yeah yeah absolutely  yeah yeah definitely um so i had um  while i was at this yale center for  british art i had the opportunity to  work with a graduate research assistant  on this project kind of investigating  objects and trying to find i had already  kind of conceived the mineralogy project  on my own and i wanted um  the kind of next stage to emerge more  collaboratively with other partners at  yale  um  the the student that i worked with menon  go day um  we eventually came up with an idea  together that was fully kind of  co-conceived such that  we're now kind of  co-co-curators fully equal collaborators  on the next stage of this um which we're  calling well it's a provisional title  right now but we're calling it the  octants journey and so we've now we've  honed in on an 18th century navigational  instrument that's in the peabody's  scientific instruments collection  um this is an object that's used to  establish latitude at sea  um and we are working  as part of an exhibition on scientific  instruments it's going to happen at the  art gallery the yale university art  gallery in a couple of years um we're  going to be doing a kind of intervention  into that exhibition where this where  this navigational instrument basically  travels around the exhibition  encountering objects in it and narrating  its own history um in a printed booklet  and on social media and we're what we're  doing is we're playfully adapting an  18th century literary genre called the  it narrative where  objects that are kind of circulating  around the world um are imbued with  narrative power and tell their own  stories um and so we're kind of we're  imagining the octent this this  navigational instrument as a um as a  kind of stand-in for the museum visitor  so we're dramatizing the process by  which meaning is made in a museum  through these encounters between the  octane and these objects and the idea is  that as it explores objects at the  gallery it is  questioning its own role as a kind of  navigational instrument from this period  of imperial expansion thinking about its  own role in astronomical expeditions  um  genocide and enslavement  kind of  extractive colonialism  and yeah just trying to kind of  understand its own implication in those  histories  so that's a kind of you know it's a very  different sort of a project than this  but like this the idea is to kind of  bring objects that don't seem to have  anything to do with each other  um and use them to really question the  categories that have separated them in  museums you know because the yale  museums  do a lot of collaborative work together  but still fundamentally they have these  different kind of conventions of display  and ways of giving meaning to objects  that  illuminate them deeply but also hide  certain aspects of them and i think one  of the great possibilities of  interdisciplinary humanistic work at a  university like yale that's had a couple  of years of collecting history is that  they're it's not just that the  collections are rich and deep as  resources for our own work but they are  an invitation  to um kind of turn the lens on the  institution themselves and ask really  important questions about well why is  this object here why has it been  identified as this kind of object rather  than another kind of object and what  other sorts of histories might it tell  yeah i mean that's that's the hope  that's the hope right is to um kind of  by collaborating with the different  museums start to  inspire  changes and kind of experiments and how  they do things  i think i mean maybe another way of  describing what i'm after in this  project is that um  i think many now i'm not talking about  yale i'm talking about um kind of  european and american museums more  broadly um  the place where this kind of kind of  creative  and playful and kind of unexpected work  happens is through artist interventions  right like people invite the artist to  come in engage with the collection and  then either make work or select work in  a way that  kind of cuts across  the categories that have structured that  collection and i really believe in that  work and i find it really powerful but  my worry about it is that it segregates  that kind of critical work and says that  this is the work of the artist the  institution kind of follows its own  um  kind of dynamic that is that's not a  part of that and so you end up  displaying these kinds of interventions  rather than incorporating them into the  practice of the museum  um i don't  so i'm i'm really interested in finding  way you know as a curator i was trying  to do that from within i'm now  working from without again but also very  much with museums and so i  my hope is that we start to really  yeah change change the way these objects  meaning is displayed in all these  different spaces  thanks  [Music]  you
 

